      "source": [
        "## 10. Conclusion and unresolved risk\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, the MLP was the better of the two neural models under the same split, windows, loss and metrics. It had a lower test MAE than the CNN (31.58 ± 2.33 vs 33.33 ± 2.04 Wh) and trained faster (1.9 s vs 2.6 s). RMSE and R² were essentially tied (68.04 vs 67.73 Wh and 0.441 vs 0.446), and the gap is within the variation between seeds. Neither network beat the naive last-value baseline on MAE (26.65 Wh), though. Ridge was the only model to improve on the naive RMSE, reaching 61.22 Wh (7.9% better). The target sits around 50 to 60 Wh most of the time with short spikes, so repeating the last value is hard to beat on MAE at a 10-minute horizon. Our Huber loss (δ = 1 on the standardised target, about 100 Wh) also behaves like MSE for almost every error. That pushes the networks toward predicting the average, which helps on the spikes (RMSE) but costs MAE.\n",
        "\n",
        "The grid search showed that the shortest window of 18 steps (3 hours) gave the best validation MAE for the MLP, CNN and Ridge. Validation MAE got worse as the window grew: the MLP went from 29.57 to 31.67 to 34.71 Wh at 18, 36 and 72 steps. Adding dropout of 0.2 improved the MLP in all 18 matched configurations, which suggests most of the useful signal is in recent history and the larger models overfit without regularisation.\n",
        "\n",
        "One unresolved risk is that the dataset only covers about 4.5 months (January to May 2016). The test period is on average 8.6 °C warmer than training, so the models already see input values outside the training range. The target stayed stable through this shift, but we have not tested how the models behave in summer or across a full year.\n",
        "\n",
        "For Stage 2 we will add the RNN, LSTM/GRU and TCN models under the same protocol. Since the best window sat at the edge of the grid, we will also try windows shorter than 18 steps, and we will test an L1 loss so training matches the MAE metric used for selection.\n",
        "\n",
      ]
    }
